\begin{minipage}[t]{0.81\linewidth}
Calculate the mole fraction of ethylene glycol (C$_2$H$_6$O$_2$) in a solution containing 20\% of C$_2$H$_6$O$_2$ by mass.
\end{minipage}

\vspace{8pt}
\noindent\begin{minipage}[t]{0.17\linewidth}
\raggedleft{\color{DeepSkyBlue3}\large\itshape\underline{Solution}}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.81\linewidth}
Assume that we have 100 g of solution (one can start with any amount of solution because the results obtained will be the same). Solution will contain 20 g of ethylene glycol and 80 g of water.

Molar mass of C$_2$H$_6$O$_2$ = 12 $\times$ 2 + 1 $\times$ 6 + 16 $\times$ 2 = 62 g mol$^{-1}$.
\[ \text{Moles of C}_2\text{H}_6\text{O}_2 = \frac{20\ \text{g}}{62\ \text{g mol}^{-1}} = 0.322\ \text{mol} \]
\[ \text{Moles of water} = \frac{80\ \text{g}}{18\ \text{g mol}^{-1}} = 4.444\ \text{mol} \]
\[ \begin{aligned}
\text{x}_{\text{glycol}} &= \frac{\text{moles of C}_2\text{H}_6\text{O}_2}{\text{moles of C}_2\text{H}_6\text{O}_2 + \text{moles of H}_2\text{O}}\\[4pt]
&= \frac{0.322\ \text{mol}}{0.322\ \text{mol} + 4.444\ \text{mol}} = 0.068
\end{aligned} \]
\[ \text{Similarly, } x_{\text{water}} = \frac{4.444\ \text{mol}}{0.322\ \text{mol} + 4.444\ \text{mol}} = 0.932 \]
Mole fraction of water can also be calculated as: 1 -- 0.068 = 0.932
\end{minipage}
\end{tcolorbox}

\vspace{4pt}
\noindent\hfill\begin{minipage}[t]{0.74\textwidth}
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=24pt,topsep=0pt,start=6]
\item \textit{Molarity}: Molarity (\textit{M}) is defined as number of moles of solute dissolved in one litre (or one cubic decimetre) of solution,
\[ \text{Molarity} = \frac{\text{Moles of solute}}{\text{Volume of solution in litre}} \tag{1.8} \]
\end{enumerate}
\hspace*{2.5em}For example, 0.25 mol L$^{-1}$ (or 0.25 M) solution of NaOH means that 0.25 mol of NaOH has been dissolved in one litre (or one cubic decimetre).
\end{minipage}

