\noindent\begin{minipage}[t]{0.17\linewidth}
\raggedleft{\color{DeepSkyBlue3}\large\itshape\underline{Solution}}
\end{minipage}\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.81\linewidth}
The solubility of gas is related to the mole fraction in aqueous solution. The mole fraction of the gas in the solution is calculated by applying Henry's law. Thus:

\noindent$\displaystyle x\,\text{(Nitrogen)} \;=\; \frac{p\ \text{(nitrogen)}}{K_\text{H}} = \frac{0.987\text{bar}}{76{,}480\ \text{bar}} = 1.29 \times 10^{-5}$

As 1 litre of water contains 55.5 mol of it, therefore if \textit{n} represents number of moles of N$_2$ in solution,

\noindent$\displaystyle x\,\text{(Nitrogen)} = \frac{n\ \text{mol}}{n\ \text{mol} + 55.5\ \text{mol}} = \frac{n}{55.5} = 1.29 \times 10^{-5}$

(\textit{n} in denominator is neglected as it is $<<$ 55.5)\\
Thus $n$ = 1.29 $\times$ 10$^{-5}$ $\times$ 55.5 mol = 7.16 $\times$ 10$^{-4}$ mol

\noindent$\displaystyle = \frac{7.16{\times}10^{-4}\ \text{mol} \times 1000\ \text{mmol}}{1\ \text{mol}} = 0.716\ \text{mmol}$
\end{minipage}
\end{tcolorbox}

