\hspace*{1.5em}The expression on the left hand side of the equation as mentioned earlier is called \textbf{relative lowering of vapour pressure and is equal to the mole fraction of the solute}. The above equation can be written as:
\[ \frac{p_1^{\,0} - p_1}{p_1^{\,0}} \;=\; \frac{n_2}{n_1 + n_2} \quad \left(\text{since } x_2 = \frac{n_2}{n_1 + n_2}\right) \tag{1.26} \]
\hspace*{1.5em}Here $n_1$ and $n_2$ are the number of moles of solvent and solute respectively present in the solution. For dilute solutions $n_2 << n_1$, hence neglecting $n_2$ in the denominator we have
\[ \frac{p_1^{\,0} - p_1}{p_1^{\,0}} \;=\; \frac{n_2}{n_1} \tag{1.27} \]
\[ \text{or}\quad \frac{p_1^{\,0} - p_1}{p_1^{\,0}} \;=\; \frac{\text{w}_2 \times M_1}{M_2 \times \text{w}_1} \tag{1.28} \]
\hspace*{1.5em}Here $w_1$ and $w_2$ are the masses and $M_1$ and $M_2$ are the molar masses of the solvent and solute respectively.

\hspace*{1.5em}From this equation (1.28), knowing all other quantities, the molar mass of solute ($M_2$) can be calculated.
