\hspace*{1.5em}Let $T_\text{b}^{\,0}$ be the boiling point of pure solvent and $T_\text{b}$ be the boiling point of solution. The increase in the boiling point $\Delta T_\text{b} = T_\text{b} - T_\text{b}^{\,0}$ is known as \textbf{elevation of boiling point}.

\hspace*{1.5em}Experiments have shown that for \textbf{dilute solutions} the elevation of boiling point ($\Delta T_\text{b}$) is directly proportional to the molal concentration of the solute in a solution. Thus

\hspace*{3.4em}$\Delta T_\text{b} \propto \text{m}$ \hfill (1.29)

