\noindent\hfill\begin{minipage}[t]{0.765\textwidth}
\textbf{Depression Constant or Cryoscopic Constant}. The unit of K$_\text{f}$ is K kg mol$^{-1}$. Values of $K_\text{f}$ for some common solvents are listed in Table 1.3.

\hspace*{1.5em}If w$_2$ gram of the solute having molar mass as $M_2$, present in w$_1$ gram of solvent, produces the depression in freezing point $\Delta T_\text{f}$ of the solvent then molality of the solute is given by the equation (1.31).
\[ \text{m} \;=\; \frac{w_2\ /\ M_2}{w_1/1000} \tag{1.31} \]
\hspace*{1.5em}Substituting this value of molality in equation (1.34) we get:
\begin{align*}
\Delta T_\text{f} &= \frac{K_\text{f}\ \times\ w_2\,/\,M_2}{w_1\,/1000}\\
\Delta T_\text{f} &= \frac{K_\text{f} \times w_2 \times 1000}{M_2 \times w_1} \tag{1.35}\\
M_2 &= \frac{K_\text{f} \times w_2 \times 1000}{\Delta T_\text{f} \times w_1} \tag{1.36}
\end{align*}
\hspace*{1.5em}Thus for determining the molar mass of the solute we should know the quantities w$_1$, w$_2$, $\Delta T_\text{f}$, along with the molal freezing point depression constant.

\hspace*{1.5em}The values of $K_\text{f}$ and $K_\text{b}$, which depend upon the nature of the solvent, can be ascertained from the following relations.
\begin{align*}
K_\text{f} &= \frac{R \times M_1 \times T_\text{f}^2}{1000 \times \Delta_{\text{fus}}H} \tag{1.37}\\
K_\text{b} &= \frac{R \times M_1 \times T_\text{b}^2}{1000 \times \Delta_{\text{vap}}H} \tag{1.38}
\end{align*}
\hspace*{1.5em}Here the symbols $R$ and $M_1$ stand for the gas constant and molar mass of the solvent, respectively and $T_\text{f}$ and $T_\text{b}$ denote the freezing point and the boiling point of the pure solvent respectively in kelvin. Further, $\Delta_{\text{fus}}H$ and $\Delta_{\text{vap}}H$ represent the enthalpies for the fusion and vapourisation of the solvent, respectively.
\end{minipage}

